 ## Overview
-Atomized Proliferation is a training optimization strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that enhances geometric precision by constraining Gaussians representing fine details into "Atom Gaussians" (isotropic spheroids with uniform size) and prioritizing their proliferation to align with scene geometry [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard 3DGS densification by using a warm-up phase that aggressively densifies initially, followed by merging of large smooth surfaces, leading to a stabilizing Gaussian count and superior mesh quality [S1, unofficial].
+Atomized Proliferation is a training optimization strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that enhances geometric precision by constraining Gaussians representing fine details into "Atom Gaussians" (isotropic spheroids with uniform size) and prioritizing their proliferation to align with scene geometry [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard 3D Gaussian Splatting, which struggles to stabilize densification and often produces noisy meshes or blurry artifacts, Atomized Proliferation uses a warm-up strategy that aggressively densifies initially, then merges large smooth surfaces, leading to a declining and stabilizing trend in Gaussian count [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Gaussian Representation and Truncation](12855-gaussian-representation-and-truncation.md) by introducing specific atomization and geometry-guided pruning mechanisms.
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** A set of 3D Gaussians defined by mean $\mu$, covariance $\Sigma$, color $c$, and opacity $\alpha$; input Structure-from-Motion (SfM) points; hyperparameters $\tau_c, \tau_s, \epsilon, S, t_a, t_w$.
-*   **Outputs:** An optimized set of Gaussians where fine details are represented by fixed-size Atom Gaussians and smooth surfaces are merged.
